    <main class="doc">
    <h1>Darshan · Walkthrough</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      A pluggable, browser-only vision-agent shell. One UI hosts swappable pipelines on top of
      swappable inference backends — all running locally in your tab.
    </p>

    <h2 id="quickstart">1. Quickstart</h2>

    <p>Three commands:</p>
    <pre><code>cd darshan
bun install
bun run build:wasm   # one-time: compiles Rust → wasm32 (≈10s)
bun dev              # → http://localhost:4321</code></pre>

    <p>Then open <a href="/run">/run</a>, pick a pipeline and a backend, and drop a file.</p>

    <p><strong>Requirements:</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Bun 1.3+</strong> for the JS workspace.</li>
      <li><strong>Rustup with the <code>wasm32-unknown-unknown</code> target</strong> for the Candle (WASM) backend.
        <code>rustup target add wasm32-unknown-unknown</code> if missing.</li>
      <li><strong>WebGPU-capable browser</strong> for full speed: Chrome 121+, Edge, Arc, Brave, or Safari 26+.
        Firefox and older Safari fall back to WASM (~3× slower).</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="concepts">2. Concepts</h2>

    <h3>Pipeline</h3>
    <p>
      A <code>Pipeline&lt;TInput, TOutput&gt;</code> is a task. It takes a file (or video), runs whatever
      detection / recognition / scoring logic it needs, and yields a typed result. Pipelines compose
      one or more loaded models behind their own <code>load</code> / <code>process</code> / <code>dispose</code> lifecycle.
    </p>
    <p>The three pipelines today:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>echo</strong> — seam test. Reads the dropped file, returns sha256 + metadata. No ML.</li>
      <li><strong>ocr</strong> — Devanagari OCR + Hindi→English translation. Proposes text regions, recognizes each with Tesseract.js (Hindi traineddata), translates with Opus-MT via transformers.js.</li>
      <li><strong>dashcam</strong> — driving-clip incident extractor. Samples 5 FPS, runs YOLOv10n, scores looming-vehicle / pedestrian-close / traffic-control runs, emits clip ranges.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Backend</h3>
    <p>
      An <code>InferenceBackend</code> is a runtime — it knows how to fetch a <code>ModelSpec</code>, load
      weights, and run the resulting model on a tensor or image. Pipelines never call backends directly
      with framework specifics; they receive a <code>LoadedModel</code>.
    </p>
    <p>The four backends today:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Mock</strong> — deterministic JS, no real ML. Proves the seam end-to-end.</li>
      <li><strong>transformers.js</strong> — <code>@huggingface/transformers</code> v4. WebGPU preferred, WASM fallback. Powers the OCR pipeline's translation step (Opus-MT-hi-en). Recognition itself uses Tesseract.js — outside the backend abstraction because Tesseract has its own worker pool and traineddata format, not a HuggingFace <code>ModelSpec</code>.</li>
      <li><strong>ORT Web</strong> — <code>onnxruntime-web</code> with WebGPU EP. Powers dashcam (raw ONNX YOLO).</li>
      <li><strong>Candle (WASM)</strong> — Rust crate compiled to <code>wasm32-unknown-unknown</code>, loaded as a raw WebAssembly module without wasm-bindgen. Today the Rust <code>infer()</code> body is a tagged echo; phase 5 swaps it for <code>candle-core</code> under the same ABI.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>The seam</h3>
    <p>
      The whole project is about the boundary between Pipeline and Backend. The
      <code>InferenceBackend</code> interface is identical whether it wraps transformers.js, ORT, or a
      hand-rolled Rust crate. The UI has no idea which one is loaded. This is what the Candle (WASM)
      backend proves: the same TypeScript can call into Rust over linear memory without any framework
      assumptions baked into the shell.
    </p>

    <h3>Worker offload</h3>
    <p>
      Echo and OCR run inside a dedicated <code>Worker</code>. The main thread sees only a
      <code>PipelineClient</code> proxy that posts messages and resolves promises. Network and inference
      progress stream back as <code>progress</code> events on the same channel. Dashcam is the exception
      — it decodes <code>&lt;video&gt;</code> which has no equivalent in <code>DedicatedWorkerGlobalScope</code>,
      so it runs on the main thread. GPU compute still goes through the WebGPU execution provider, so
      the main thread blocks only on per-frame glue (negligible).
    </p>

    <h3>Cache API privacy proof</h3>
    <p>
      Model weights are fetched once and cached locally — never re-fetched. transformers.js writes
      to <code>caches.open('darshan-models-v1')</code> for its HuggingFace downloads (Opus-MT, YOLO
      ONNX). Tesseract.js writes <code>hin.traineddata</code> to its own IndexedDB cache. To verify
      privacy hands-on: load <a href="/run">/run</a>, run an OCR pass to populate both caches,
      enable airplane mode, drop a different image — it still works.
    </p>

    <h3>Capabilities row</h3>
    <p>
      At the bottom of <a href="/run">/run</a>, three pills show <code>webgpu</code> / <code>webgl</code> / <code>wasm</code> ✓/✗ for this browser.
      If <code>webgpu ✗</code>, transformers.js and ORT both fall back to WASM EP automatically — slower
      but works everywhere.
    </p>

    <h2 id="matrix">3. Pipeline × Backend matrix</h2>

    <p>The Backend picker auto-disables incompatible options once a pipeline is selected:</p>

    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Pipeline</th><th>Mock</th><th>transformers.js</th><th>ORT Web</th><th>Candle (WASM)</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>echo</td>
          <td><span class="yes">✓ default</span></td>
          <td><span class="no">disabled</span></td>
          <td><span class="no">disabled</span></td>
          <td><span class="seam">✓ seam proof</span></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>ocr</td>
          <td><span class="no">disabled</span></td>
          <td><span class="yes">✓ default</span></td>
          <td><span class="no">disabled</span></td>
          <td><span class="no">disabled</span></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>dashcam</td>
          <td><span class="no">disabled</span></td>
          <td><span class="no">disabled</span></td>
          <td><span class="yes">✓ default</span></td>
          <td><span class="no">disabled</span></td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3>echo + Mock</h3>
    <p>Worker → fake load (~100ms) → reads dropped file → sha256 + mime + bytes. Verifies the worker pipeline end-to-end without any real ML. Zero network.</p>

    <h3>echo + Candle (WASM)</h3>
    <p>
      Worker → fetches <code>/wasm/darshan-inference-core.wasm</code> (~10 kB, one-time) → instantiates raw
      module → reads first 64 KB of the dropped file → passes to Rust <code>infer()</code>. The Rust side returns
      <code>darshan/wasm:&lt;version&gt;:&lt;input bytes&gt;</code>; the EchoPipeline discards that and emits its own
      sha256 summary. The seam ran for real.
    </p>

    <h3>ocr + transformers.js</h3>
    <p>
      Worker → lazy-imports two runtimes: <code>tesseract.js</code> for recognition and
      <code>@huggingface/transformers</code> for translation. First run downloads:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Tesseract Hindi traineddata</strong> (<code>hin.traineddata</code>, ~5 MB) — actually trained on Devanagari, unlike English-only TrOCR.</li>
      <li><code>Xenova/opus-mt-hi-en</code> (~80 MB) — Hindi → English translation, routed through the standard <code>InferenceBackend</code> seam.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Then per dropped image: <code>createImageBitmap</code> → horizontal-projection region proposer
      (Devanagari shirorekha-aware) → per-region crop → Tesseract recognize → Opus-MT translate →
      SVG overlay with saffron-orange bboxes. Click a box (or list item) to reveal recognized +
      translated text for that region.
    </p>
    <div class="callout">
      <p><strong>Recognizer choice.</strong> v0.2 used Microsoft TrOCR via transformers.js — but TrOCR-small-printed was trained on Latin script and produced receipt-shaped hallucinations on real Devanagari. v0.3 swapped to Tesseract.js with <code>hin.traineddata</code>, which is actually trained on the script. Printed Devanagari now works well; handwriting is still imperfect — no engine, online or offline, handles cursive Devanagari with high accuracy. The translator stays as transformers.js Opus-MT so the seam claim still holds for that half of the pipeline. Configure with <code>new OcrPipeline(&lbrace; recognizerLanguage: 'hin+eng' &rbrace;)</code> to mix Hindi + Latin pages.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>dashcam + ORT Web</h3>
    <p>
      Main thread (not worker — needs <code>&lt;video&gt;</code>) → lazy-imports <code>onnxruntime-web</code> →
      first run downloads <code>onnx-community/yolov10n</code> int8 (~2.6 MB). Per dropped clip:
      HTMLVideoElement decodes → sampled at 5 FPS via <code>requestVideoFrameCallback</code> →
      letterbox 640×640 CHW Float32 → ORT (WebGPU EP) → YOLOv10 decode (NMS-free, trim by
      score) → un-letterbox → per-frame summary → incident scoring run-length.
    </p>
    <p>
      Result: video player with timeline bar overlaying colored incident marks (orange = looming
      vehicle, red = pedestrian close, yellow = traffic control). Click a mark to seek.
    </p>

    <h2 id="files">4. What file to drop</h2>

    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Pipeline</th><th>File types</th><th>What works best</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>echo</td>
          <td>any</td>
          <td>Any file under ~100 MB. Bytes are touched only for sha256 + (with WASM) the first 64 KB.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>ocr</td>
          <td>JPG / PNG / WEBP</td>
          <td>Clean scan of Devanagari text — dark ink on light background, ~600 px tall, distinct horizontal lines. Tesseract handles printed Devanagari well; cursive handwriting is genuinely hard for every OCR engine.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>dashcam</td>
          <td>MP4 / WEBM / MOV</td>
          <td>720p dashcam-angle driving footage, 5–60 s. Cars, pedestrians, stop signs in frame. Avoid 4K — sampling cost scales with source resolution.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2 id="flow">5. Request flow</h2>

    <p>OCR run, top-to-bottom:</p>

    <pre><code>┌─ /run page (Astro, Preact island) ─────────────────────────┐
│  Shell.tsx                                                  │
│    DropZone ──onFile──► PipelineClient.process()            │
│      │                                                       │
│      │ Picker(pipeline) Picker(backend) ProgressMeter        │
│      ▼                                                       │
│  postMessage &lbrace; kind:'process', fileBlob, ... &rbrace; │
└────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
              │ worker boundary
              ▼
┌─ inference worker (Web Worker) ────────────────────────────┐
│  workers/inference.ts                                       │
│    registerBackend('transformers-js', () => …)              │
│    registerPipeline('ocr', () => new OcrPipeline())         │
│    bootstrap()                                              │
│                                                              │
│  OcrPipeline.process(file)                                  │
│    createImageBitmap → proposeRegions → per-region crop     │
│       │                                                      │
│       ▼                                                      │
│    tesseract.recognize(crop)  ◄── tesseract.js (hin)         │
│       │                                                      │
│       ▼                                                      │
│    LoadedModel.run(text)      ◄── TransformersJsBackend     │
│       │                                                      │
│       ▼                                                      │
│    transformers.js Opus-MT (WebGPU EP)                       │
│       │                                                      │
│       ▼                                                      │
│    yield &lbrace; regions, ... &rbrace;   ─── postMessage(result) │
└────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
              │
              ▼
       ResultPanel + OcrCanvas SVG overlay</code></pre>

    <h2 id="layout">6. Repo layout</h2>

    <pre><code>darshan/
├─ apps/web/                              Astro + Preact + Tailwind shell
│  ├─ src/components/Shell.tsx            pipeline + backend pickers, result rendering
│  ├─ src/workers/inference.ts            worker entry; registers backends + pipelines
│  ├─ src/pages/&lbrace;index,run,docs&rbrace;.astro    landing + agent + this page
│  └─ public/wasm/darshan-inference-core.wasm   Rust/WASM artifact (built)
└─ packages/
   ├─ inference-core/                     Backend + Pipeline interfaces, worker host, mock, transformers, ort
   ├─ inference-core-wasm/                Candle backend + Rust crate (wasm32, no wasm-bindgen)
   ├─ pipeline-ocr/                       Devanagari OCR + translation (Tesseract.js + Opus-MT)
   ├─ pipeline-dashcam/                   YOLOv10n + optical-flow incident scoring + timeline
   └─ ui-kit/                             DropZone, Picker, ProgressMeter, ResultPanel, OcrCanvas, TimelineScrubber</code></pre>

    <h2 id="develop">7. Develop</h2>

    <pre><code># lint everything (biome)
bun run lint

# run all package tests (bun test)
bun --filter '*' test

# build the static site
bun --filter @darshan/web build

# rebuild the Rust → WASM core
bun run build:wasm</code></pre>

    <p>
      Biome handles lint + format. Single quotes, 100-char width, 2-space indent, semicolons.
      No ESLint, no Prettier.
    </p>

    <p>
      All packages are <code>type: "module"</code> with <code>main: "./src/index.ts"</code> and no build step
      — Vite + Bun read the TypeScript directly. The only thing that compiles is the Rust crate.
    </p>

    <h2 id="troubleshooting">8. Troubleshooting</h2>

    <h3>OCR returns empty or low-quality recognized text</h3>
    <p>
      You're on Candle (WASM) — that backend doesn't do OCR (yet). Switch to transformers.js.
      If you're already on transformers.js with a Devanagari image: the recognizer is Tesseract
      with <code>hin.traineddata</code> — it handles printed Devanagari well but struggles on
      cursive handwriting. The regions in the saffron overlay are still correct; the text just
      won't be perfect.
    </p>
    <p>
      For other scripts: pass <code>new OcrPipeline(&lbrace; recognizerLanguage: 'eng' &rbrace;)</code>
      for English, <code>'hin+eng'</code> for mixed pages, etc. See the <a href="https://github.com/naptha/tessdata">Tesseract language packs</a> list.
    </p>

    <h3>Dashcam stalls on a 4K clip</h3>
    <p>
      Re-encode to 720p first, or wait — ORT WebGPU EP can handle it, but per-frame YOLO scales with
      input size after the letterbox downscale. Phase 5 work would add WebCodecs for parallel
      frame decode.
    </p>

    <h3>“unsupported input type” from transformers.js</h3>
    <p>
      This was a real bug — fixed in May 2026 by passing inputs positionally and converting images
      to data URLs. After the Tesseract swap, transformers.js only sees text inputs for translation,
      so this class of bug is no longer reachable through OCR. If you still see it (e.g., calling
      <code>TransformersJsBackend</code> directly), paste the full console stack.
    </p>

    <h3>Tesseract hangs at "loading language traineddata"</h3>
    <p>
      <code>tesseract.js</code> fetches <code>hin.traineddata</code> (~5 MB) from
      <a href="https://tessdata.projectnaptha.com/4.0.0">tessdata.projectnaptha.com</a> on first
      run. If your network blocks that origin, override with
      <code>new OcrPipeline(&lbrace; recognizerLanguage: 'hin' &rbrace;)</code> after vendoring the
      traineddata locally and pointing <code>createWorker</code>'s <code>langPath</code> at it. The
      pipeline can also bundle the data into <code>public/</code> if you want a fully self-hosted
      build.
    </p>

    <h3>WASM 404</h3>
    <p>
      Run <code>bun run build:wasm</code>. The artifact must land at <code>apps/web/public/wasm/darshan-inference-core.wasm</code>.
      It's not checked into git; you build it locally.
    </p>

    <h3>ORT Web: "no available backend found"</h3>
    <p>
      Both EPs (WebGPU + WASM) failed to load <code>onnxruntime-web</code>'s runtime
      <code>.wasm</code> blob. Root cause: Vite's dep optimizer was pre-bundling
      <code>onnxruntime-web</code> into <code>/node_modules/.vite/deps/</code>, which broke the
      sibling-path lookup ORT uses (<code>ort.bundle.min.mjs</code> dynamic-imports
      <code>./ort-wasm-simd-threaded.jsep.mjs</code> which itself does
      <code>new URL('./...wasm', import.meta.url)</code>). Fix: <code>optimizeDeps.exclude:
      ['onnxruntime-web']</code> in <code>astro.config.mjs</code> so ORT stays in its native
      layout. We also pin <code>ort.env.wasm.numThreads = 1</code> to skip the multi-threaded
      build (which needs COOP+COEP cross-origin-isolation headers we don't set).
    </p>

    <h3>“No WebGPU adapter”</h3>
    <p>
      Some VMs, remote desktops, and older mobile browsers gate WebGPU behind a flag. Everything
      still works via WASM fallback at roughly 3× slowdown.
    </p>
